Parkes2021.pdf.md:172:## Limitations
Parkes2021.pdf.md:174:A limitation of this study is the use of cross-sectional data to model neurodevelopment. It is well documented that individual variability in neurodevelopment occurs at both the inter-individual and intra-individual level 2 , and characterizing the factors that explain the latter will be critical for predicting the emergence of psychopathology over time. Thus, future work should test whether the brain regions identi /uniFB01 ed using our approach explain variance in psychopathology dimensions at follow-up time points. Another limitation is the focus on T1-weighted cortical brain features. The underlying white matter pathways change throughout development in parallel with the cortex, giving rise to segregated processing modules that increase the functional ef /uniFB01 ciency of the brain 58 -60 . Here, we focused on cortical volume derived from T1weighted imaging data owing to the robust relationship with age, which is well suited to normative modeling. Future work that builds multimodal normative models of neurodevelopment may provide additional insights into the physiology of psychopathology, which is critical to progressing the /uniFB01 eld of psychiatry towards personalized medicine.
Parkes2021.pdf.md:176:Finally, we note that controlling for socioeconomic status and general intelligence in our predictive models resulted in many of our prediction models dropping below signi /uniFB01 cance under our permutation test (see Supplementary Materials), suggesting that these measures confounded our prediction models. However, we note that predictive performance of overall psychopathology from deviations was still signi /uniFB01 cantly higher than from raw brain volume, suggesting that the bene /uniFB01 t of using the normative model persisted. Nevertheless, this result suggests future work should examine the extent to which socioeconomic status and general intelligence might mediate the relationship between deviations from normative neurodevelopment and psychopathology.
Parkes2021.pdf.md:180:Our results represent an important step toward understanding the link between neurodevelopment and psychopathology. We explicitly modeled normative variance in neurodevelopment, allowing us to estimate multivariate continuous single-subject neurodevelopmental abnormalities. Combining this approach with a dimensional model of psychopathology allowed us to improve the out-of-sample prediction of psychiatric symptoms compared to raw cortical volume, particularly for overall psychopathology. This result provides proof of principle that recasting structural neuroimaging data obtained from one sample as deviations from a separate normative cohort can improve the predictive relationship between brain and symptom data. This is encouraging because it supports the viability of generating standardized normative models that may then be deployed to understand variance in brain data acquired across multiple different clinical sites. However, this interpretation remains speculative, since our analysis was restricted to a single site. In order to examine whether the improved predictive performance observed here generalizes to new samples, future work should consider using a normative model trained on one site to understand variance in brain data acquired from a different site. Indeed, new methods from normative modeling are being developed that may facilitate this goal 61 .
Parkes2021.pdf.md:188:number of such papers in the /uniFB01 eld 62 -66 . Here we sought to proactively consider choosing references that re /uniFB02 ect the diversity of the /uniFB01 eld in thought, form of contribution, gender, race, ethnicity, and other factors. First, we obtained the predicted gender of the /uniFB01 rst and last author of each reference by using databases that store the probability of a /uniFB01 rst name being carried by a woman 66,67 . By this measure (and excluding self-citations to the /uniFB01 rst and last authors of our current paper), our references contain 15.19% woman( /uniFB01 rst)/woman(last), 16.24% man/ woman, 22.1% woman/man, and 46.46% man/man. This method is limited in that (a) names, pronouns, and social media pro /uniFB01 les used to construct the databases may not, in every case, be indicative of gender identity and (b) it cannot account for intersex, non-binary, or transgender people. Second, we obtained predicted racial/ethnic category of the /uniFB01 rst and last author of each reference by databases that store the probability of a /uniFB01 rst and last name being carried by an author of color 68,69 . By this measure (and excluding self-citations), our references contain 13.06% author of color ( /uniFB01 rst)/author of color(last), 22.88% white author/author of color, 18.62% author of color/ white author, and 45.44% white author/white author. This method is limited in that (a) names and Wikipedia pro /uniFB01 les used to make the predictions may not be indicative of racial/ethnic identity, and (b) it cannot account for Indigenous and mixed-race authors, or those who may face differential biases due to the ambiguous racialization or ethnicization of their names. We look forward to future work that could help us to better understand how to support equitable practices in science.
Parkes2021.pdf.md:276:52. Varoquaux, G. et al. Assessing and tuning brain decoders: cross-validation, caveats, and guidelines. NeuroImage 145 , 166 -179 (2017).
